Introduction

Concursus Yielding Metadata for BUILDing & Real-estate

Purpose

This document aims to outline the core features of CymBuild from the user''''s perspective, ensuring smooth software use and serving as a helpful reference when needed. It starts with an overview of the platform, helping users navigate CymBuild''''s layout with ease. Then, the document introduces essential components like tables, buttons, and filters. This introduction is crucial for two reasons: firstly, these elements are the building blocks of the application, present throughout various sections. Secondly, understanding them ensures that users can utilize them effectively.

Once the basics are understood, users should feel confident navigating the system. The next steps are to:

Explain how to carry out various processes, such as creating an inquiry or quote.

Provide a troubleshooting guide.

Offer answers to frequently asked questions.

Finally, a comprehensive list of available resources will be included.

OverviewWhen launching the application, the user is greeted by the Home/Dashboard page. The navigation menu is located on the left side, while the quick access sections are displayed on the right.

Navigation Bar

Figure 1 – Navigation Bar

Home:

Directs the user to the dashboard.

My Work:

Displays all jobs associated with the user.

Displays Actions where the user is the current assignee.

Projects:


Projects link multiple related Enquires, Quotes and Jobs of a real-world project. These may belong to one of more clients or agents but are part of the same physical construction project.

Schedule:

Opens a calendar where the user can find upcoming events, they need to be aware of. (Note: As of the writing of this document, Outlook Calendar has not yet been synced.)

CRM:

Customer Relationship Management: Shows a list of all customers in the system.

Structures:

(Properties/UPRN) Provides a list of sites where work has been conducted by SOCOTEC. It includes site addresses, unique property reference numbers (UPRNs), authorities, and more.

Enquiries:

Displays a list of all received enquiries, regardless of their status. This is the initial stage of job creation and is always required. An enquiry progresses t'
+ N'o a quote.

Quoting:

Shows all quotes available in the system. Quotes originate from enquiries and can convert into jobs.

Jobs:

Provides a list of all jobs added. Jobs are the final stage after enquiries and quotes.

Shows a list of projects with all the relevant information pertaining to them, which are generated from completed quotes.

Finance:

Lists previous transactions throughout the system, as well as those specific to the user. Users can also export invoices for importing into Sage.

Settings:

Provides access to various system settings.

Quick Access Links

On the right side of the home page, there''''s a table called "Recent" (). Its purpose is to help users keep track of previously viewed records, acting like a history. For instance, Figure 2 shows that the user previously viewed two job records, one with the ID 8524 and another with 8523.

The table is interactive: double-clicking a record of interest will automatically redirect the user to that record''''s page in CymBuild, where they can view the entire record and edit it if needed.

The document is intended to outline the essential features of CymBuild from the user''''s viewpoint, facilitating smoother software utilization and offering reference as necessary. Starting with the overview of the platform, the user is given an introduction to CymBuild’s layout

Figure 2 - Recent Table

.

Below this (Figure 3), the user can find the table called “Schedule” – this keeps track of any upcoming/ongoing action that is linked to a particular job. As such, it is only displayed when a particular action type is selected.

Figure 3- Schedule Table.

NOTE:

Scheduler is linked to activity types. You can double check if an activity is going to trigger the scheduler by going to Settings > Activity Types. Check if the “Is Schedule Item” is ticked for that activity.

To the right of the "Recent" table, there are various "meters" (technically referred to as gauges), displaying progress related to processes like jobs, invoices, and appointments.

Figure 4 - Gauges.

These gauges also operate as a quick access links to help with ease of navigation. For this, we can use the eye icon        found by the title (The icon is briefly covered here). When clicked, the user is redirected to appropriate page.

IT Support Section

At the bottom of the page, you will find a section of text that almost blends into the background – this section is dedicated to IT support as it provides information specific to the user environment, which can be used for troubleshooting issues users might encounter (Figure 5).

Figure 5 - Information for IT Support.

Modal View

A modal is a type of user interface component that appears over a web page. It is typically used for entering details related to a record but can also serve as a window for read-only information (Figure 6).

Figure 6 - Modal Window Highlighted in Red

Grid Views

When working with lists, users will typically interact with them via tables (also known as grids). The figure below provides a screenshot of one, with the different sections color-coded (Figure 7).

Figure 7- Table.

.

Dynamic Grid

The outer window that contains all the other components.

Grid Drawers:

Like items in the navigation bar, these organize data into logical sections. They can be expanded or collapsed by clicking the hamburger icon in the top-left corner.

Grid View:

Displays rows of data (as seen in Figure 9).

Grids/Rows

Figure 8 - Grid View

Each grid can be interacted with by double-clicking on it, but the outcome depends on the grid''''s specific configuration. Users can generally expect one of three behaviours:

The user is redirected to a new page.

A "modal" opens.

Nothing happens.

NOTE:

When a grid is clicked once, it just simply gets highlighted in light blue - Please, refer to Figure 9.

Filters

Tables can be sorted by each column – this means we c'
+ N'an use more than one column to sort data. To access the filter, simply locate the desired column, and click the filter icon      .  Once clicked, the dropdown below in figure 10 will appear.

Figure 9 – Grid View

Figure 10 – Filter.

To understand how this works, let''''s refer to the diagram below. The dropdown is divided into two halves. We first check if a value has been entered in the second half. If not, only the first half of the filter will be applied. Otherwise, the first and second halves are combined into a single filter, which in this diagram would be: "Contains VALUE and Ends With VALUE."

Figure 11 – Critical Path Diagram for The Filter.

Figure 12 - Filter Example

Take the screenshot above (fig. 12) as an example. Since both halves are filled out, the filter becomes:

“(Looking for values that) Contains ‘F’ and end with 10”.

If the second half isn''''t filled out, the filter will simply search for values containing the letter "F."

NOTE:

The filter is not case-sensitive, so entering values in lowercase or uppercase will not affect the results returned. After entering the filter values, the user must click the blue "Filter" button. Pressing Enter will not have any effect.

Filter Options

The previous examples rely on the same search conditions (Contains, And, Ends With, etc.), but there are additional options available. Some of the choice’s users can select include:

Is Equal To

Has No Value

Does Not Contain

Starts With

Regarding the join operator (the "And" dropdown), there are only two values:

And

Or

NOTE:

When using the "And" operator, both conditions must be true.

When using the "Or" operator, either condition can be true.

